          { name: "College-Educated Professional Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "College-educated adults represent 35% of CA-HD-8—a significant share and a nationally persuadable cohort. This group has demonstrated declining allegiance to Republican candidates in suburban districts over the past two cycles, particularly when candidates are perceived as ideologically rigid or dismissive of quality-of-life issues. A representative who engages this constituency on governance competence, environmental stewardship, and pragmatic problem-solving—not partisan ideology—can maintain their support; one who defaults to culture-war framing risks losing them to a credible Democratic alternative." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.2% renter rate means nearly one-third of the district lacks material stakes in property tax policy and has direct exposure to housing cost pressures. While renters are a minority, they represent a swing constituency whose concerns—affordability, tenant protections, access to homeownership—require specific policy attention. Dismissing renter concerns as unimportant or incompatible with Republican positioning risks allowing a Democratic challenger to mobilize this group as a credible alternative base." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54% result in a district with rapid Hispanic growth, rising renter rates, and 35% college-educated professionals is not a structural Republican majority—it is a current plurality held by Anglo homeowner consolidation and assumptions about minority voter behavior that are increasingly fragile. Any candidate who campaigns as though 54% represents a safe base without actively engaging emerging constituencies is strategically vulnerable to a challenger who invests in Hispanic turnout, college-educated persuasion, or renter mobilization." },
          { name: "Culture-War Framing Over Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 35% are college-educated professionals, 27.2% are Hispanic, and 30.2% are renters, leading with abortion restrictions, transgender issues, or other high-salience cultural divides risks activating opposition constituencies and demobilizing swing voters who prioritize economic competence and pragmatic governance. CA-HD-8 can be held by a Republican who delivers on constituent concerns; it cannot be held on cultural grounds alone against a challenger who addresses material quality-of-life issues." }
