          { name: "Renter Economics & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 42.6% renters in a $87,436 median income district, housing cost burden is the primary material reality for a plurality of constituents. Renters face eviction risk, lease instability, and landlord accountability issues that homeowners do not; any candidate who does not offer specific policy answers to rent stabilization, eviction protection, and affordable housing production will lack credibility with nearly half the district. This is not a secondary issue — it is the foundational economic concern that shapes how working renters evaluate candidates." },
          { name: "Workforce Development & Education Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 21.6% college attainment rate combined with $87,436 median income signals a working-class constituency where K-12 funding, community college affordability, and the pathway from high school to stable employment are the live educational concerns. In a younger district (34.1 median age), student debt burden and job quality are generational economic anxieties; a candidate who can speak to community college completion rates, apprenticeship access, and employer partnership more concretely than to abstract educational philosophy will connect with the actual educational reality most constituents face." },
          { name: "Asian American Economic & Safety Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.7% of the district, Asian Americans are the second-largest demographic community, but they are frequently absorbed into undifferentiated 'communities of color' messaging that misses their specific economic and public safety concerns. Asian American small business owners, renters, and workers face distinct issues around storefront safety, commercial rents, and economic mobility that differ from Hispanic or Black community concerns; treating them as a unified block rather than engaging their specific material interests means leaving persuasion opportunities unworked in a district where the margin is thin." },
          { name: "Treating Hispanic Voters as a Monolithic Bloc", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic voters at 43.7% are the largest single community and likely the margin driver of the 57% Democratic result, but they are not ideologically or economically monolithic — small business owners, renters, workers, and parents have divergent concerns around homelessness, public safety, school funding, and economic regulation. Any candidate who assumes Hispanic voters will vote Democratic as an ethnic default, rather than persuading them on material concerns, will miss real defection opportunities if challenger messaging on jobs, safety, or housing resonates." },
          { name: "Assuming the 57% Margin is Demographically Structural", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 margin in a renter-majority, working-class district with diverse ethnic communities is a working majority, not a demographic lock. In a constituency where 42.6% rent, income is $87,436, and college attainment is only 21.6%, voters are evaluating candidates on immediate material delivery — housing, jobs, schools, safety — not inherited party loyalty. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 57% as a stable floor rather than a coalition requiring active maintenance of renter engagement and economic messaging will find the margin collapse if public safety, homelessness, or school funding deteriorates." }
