          { name: "Housing Affordability & Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 33.7% renters and median household income of $112,384 in a high-cost California market, housing is the binding constraint on economic mobility for roughly one-third of the district's households. This is not aspirational policy terrain; it is material household economics. Zoning reform, development policy, rent stabilization, and tenant protections are the policy levers that directly affect whether young professionals and working families can afford to remain in the community, and they are the primary economic security concerns for a renter-plurality constituency." },
          { name: "Latino & Asian Community Economic Representation", tag: "careful", why: "Combined, Latino and Asian residents represent 46.62% of CA-HD-15's population—a clear demographic majority when paired with any portion of the Black and unaccounted-for populations. These communities are not monolithic; Latino families range from immigrant workers to established middle-class professionals, and Asian communities encompass both recent immigrants and multi-generational Americans. Treating these populations as a single voting bloc or assuming their concerns are identical to white college-educated professionals means missing the specific economic and social stakes—workplace rights and wage policy for Latino workers, education quality for Asian families with school-age children, immigration integration policy for all—that drive their electoral behavior and which differ from the priorities of white affluent professionals." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as Unstable or Requiring Persuasion of Opposition Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "The 64-36 result directly reflects CA-HD-15's demographic composition and the alignment between that composition and Democratic policy positioning on education, housing, and progressive taxation. Opposition voters at 36% are not a persuadable swing population; they are ideologically coherent conservatives in a district whose structural demography is aligned with Democratic messaging. Campaigning as though the 36% opposition represents soft Republican support or a base to convert misreads the electoral terrain and diverts resources from the actual strategic task: consolidating and maximizing turnout among the Democratic-aligned majority." },
          { name: "Assuming Educational Attainment Automatically Produces Policy Sophistication or Reduces Dissatisfaction with Service Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "The 34.1% college attainment rate signals an educated constituency, but education does not eliminate dissatisfaction with government performance on schools, housing, or economic mobility. A college-educated constituency may actually be more demanding of specific policy results and more likely to punish incumbent representatives for failure to deliver on education funding or housing affordability—areas where Democratic control of state and local government means responsibility for outcomes falls directly on Democratic officials. Assuming that education level correlates with partisan loyalty or reduced accountability is a strategic error in a district this informed." }
