          { name: "Asian-Majority Community Engagement with Cultural & Policy Specificity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 61.83% of the district population, Asian residents are the demographic majority, but they are not a monolithic voting bloc — the population includes recent immigrants with specific interests in visa policy and trade relationships, multigenerational professionals evaluating on general governance competence, and multiple ethnic communities with distinct policy concerns and cultural priorities. A representative who engages this majority with cultural fluency, who understands the differences between South Asian, East Asian, and Southeast Asian communities' policy interests, and who demonstrates substantive engagement on issues like H-1B visa reform, education access, and trade policy builds a relationship that passive demographic representation cannot create. Treating 61.83% of the constituency as interchangeable risks alienating a population that expects sophisticated cultural engagement." },
          { name: "High-Income Renter Economy & Housing Costs", tag: "careful", why: "The 37.9% renter rate is significant, but CA-HD-24's renters are predominantly high-income professionals in expensive multifamily housing — not cost-burdened tenants struggling with affordability. This population is concerned with housing supply, zoning policy, and the relationship between housing costs and household financial security at the $171k income level, but they are not demanding radical redistribution or tenant protections that would alienate the homeowner majority. The risk is conflating high-income renters' concerns with working-class housing advocacy; this constituency needs housing policy that aligns with professional-class economic interests, not class-based tenancy politics." },
          { name: "Assuming 66% Democratic Margin Provides Insulation from Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% margin in a high-education, affluent district reflects alignment on values and governance competence, not tribal loyalty; this constituency has the sophistication and resources to organize against an incumbent who is perceived as underperforming or out of step with community priorities. The 57% college-educated population is likely to hold a representative accountable on specific policy delivery and constituent service intensity — any candidate who campaigns as though 66% is a stable floor without active engagement and demonstrated results risks being surprised by a primary challenger or general election vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating Asian Constituents as a Monolithic Interest Group", tag: "avoid", why: "The 61.83% Asian population is diverse across ethnic communities, immigration status, length of residence, and professional sector — yet representatives often default to treating Asian constituents as an undifferentiated bloc for electoral purposes. This approach risks alienating specific communities whose policy concerns (visa reform for H-1B workers, trade policy impacts on family businesses, education access for first-generation immigrants) are distinct and deserve specific engagement. A representative who fails to demonstrate cultural and policy specificity in engaging the demographic majority of the district will lose credibility with the population most capable of organizing an alternative challenge." }
