        issues: [
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Delivery and Renter Household Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.7% renters and $65,569 median income, CA-HD-27 is a working-class district where housing stability, healthcare affordability, and wage adequacy are the primary household economic concerns. Soria's record on delivering for renters — tenant protections, housing affordability programs, healthcare expansion — directly determines whether the 54% coalition holds or contracts in 2026. A representative who can articulate specific wins for working-class families on these material concerns will consolidate the Democratic base; one who relies on abstract progressive positioning without concrete delivery will find the margin vulnerable to a challenger offering economic specificity." },
          { name: "Coalition Retention Across Non-Hispanic Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 33.15% of CA-HD-27 that is white (19.9%), Asian (6.5%), and Black (3.67%) represents one-third of the district and is analytically decisive — a shift of 4-5 percentage points in how these communities vote swings the seat from 54% Democratic to competitive or Republican. Understanding whether this coalition is voting Democratic on economic grounds (and therefore persuadable by credible conservative economic messaging) or on value-based opposition to Republican positions (and therefore more stable) is the foundational strategic question. Soria's ability to retain these communities requires direct constituent attention and messaging that addresses their specific economic and social concerns, not absorption into a generic Hispanic-majority narrative." },
          { name: "Understanding the 46% Opposition and Its Composition", tag: "careful", why: "In a 67% Hispanic district, a 46% opposition margin cannot be explained by demographics alone and likely reflects some combination of conservative Hispanic voters, erosion in the non-Hispanic coalition, or mobilization against specific Soria positions or record. Identifying precisely who comprises the 46% — conservative Hispanic homeowners, non-Hispanic working-class voters persuaded by Republican economic messaging, ideological Republican voters — and why they rejected Soria in 2024 is essential diagnostic work before 2026. Any incumbent campaign that treats the 54% as a durable baseline without understanding what generated the 46% risks being blindsided by a more sophisticated challenger campaign." },
          { name: "Framing Working-Class Concerns as Partisan Ideology Rather Than Material Household Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "The 14.4% college-educated rate indicates a constituency that evaluates representatives primarily on concrete economic delivery — housing, healthcare, wages — rather than on ideological positioning or values-based messaging. A campaign that frames renter protections as progressive values rather than as household economic security, or that emphasizes abstract social justice concerns over wage adequacy and healthcare access, will misalign its messaging with how this electorate actually evaluates candidates. This district responds to material propositions, not rhetorical ones." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic Turnout Will Remain Constant Without Active Mobilization", tag: "avoid", why: "At 54% in a 67% Hispanic district, Soria's margin is arithmetically dependent on Hispanic voter participation as a percentage of total turnout. Any erosion in Hispanic turnout relative to 2024 — whether from genuine demobilization, competing priorities, or improved Republican outreach — directly contracts the Democratic vote share. A campaign that assumes the Hispanic majority will turn out at 2024 rates without explicit mobilization infrastructure and messaging will find itself operating from a shrinking numerical foundation in 2026." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "CA-HD-27: A 54-46 Democratic Hold in a 67% Hispanic, Working-Class District Where Incumbent Performance and Economic Messaging Will Determine 2026 Viability.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "CA-HD-27 is a solidly Democratic district on demographic surface — 66.85% Hispanic, $65,569 median household income, 46.7% renters, and 31.5 median age all signal a younger, diverse, working-class constituency that aligns with Democratic coalition fundamentals. Soria's 54-46 win in 2024 reflects this alignment, but the margin is narrower than the district's demographic composition might predict. At 54%, this is a hold, not a mandate; in a state trending Republican and in a midterm cycle where incumbent party performance typically softens, a 54% result is the baseline from which erosion is mathematically likely. The district's Hispanic majority (66.85%) is the demographic anchor, but the 19.9% white, 6.5% Asian, and 3.67% Black communities together represent one-third of the constituency — a coalition that Soria must actively retain, not assume. The 14.4% college-educated adult rate is notably low, signaling a constituency that evaluates representatives primarily on concrete economic delivery rather than ideological positioning or abstract messaging.",
          "The strategic foundation for holding this seat rests on two connected imperatives: demonstrating tangible economic progress for working-class households and maintaining coalition discipline across the district's diverse communities. At $65,569 median income with 46.7% renters, most constituents in CA-HD-27 face direct cost-of-living pressures — housing affordability, healthcare access, wage stagnation, and childcare costs are not abstract policy concerns but material household calculations that determine whether families can stay in the district or are forced to move. Soria's record on these issues — what has he delivered for renters, what is his position on healthcare expansion, where does he stand on minimum wage and labor organizing — will be the primary metrics by which voters evaluate his 2026 reelection bid. The 46% opposition in a 67% Hispanic district is not negligible; it likely reflects either conservative Hispanic voters persuaded by economic messaging or erosion among the Asian, Black, and white communities who are evaluating on different criteria. Understanding specifically who comprises that 46% and why they rejected Soria in 2024 is the single most important diagnostic work any campaign can do before the 2026 cycle begins."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with concrete economic delivery: articulate what Soria has won for renters, what he is doing on healthcare costs, and what his record shows on wage and working-condition improvements for the district's 46.7% renter majority and working-class households at $65,569 median income.",
          "Retain the non-Hispanic coalition: the 33.15% of the district that is white (19.9%), Asian (6.5%), and Black (3.67%) is the difference between a 54% hold and a loss; understand whether this coalition is soft (persuadable on economic grounds) or hardened (ideologically committed opposition), and build messaging accordingly.",
          "Translate Hispanic majority into turnout: at 66.85% Hispanic, the district's demographic advantage is only viable if Hispanic voters turn out at rates equal to or exceeding 2024; any decline in Hispanic participation as a percentage of total votes cast materially narrows the 54% margin.",
          "Avoid abstract progressive messaging that does not connect to household economics: the 14.4% college-educated rate indicates a constituency that responds to direct material propositions — housing affordability, healthcare access, job quality — more than to values-based or identity-centered framing, regardless of how resonant those frames may be elsewhere."
        ]
