          { name: "Workforce Development and Education Pipeline for Young Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34-year median age and 18.6% college attainment rate together signal a young, working-class Latino constituency for whom education access and wage mobility are primary evaluative concerns. In a district where the median household income is $95k—solid but not prosperous—families are assessing whether their children will have genuine pathways to economic advancement or whether they face the same wage constraints as their parents. Vocational training, community college affordability, employer partnerships, and K-12 school quality are concrete governance issues that affect how young parents and workforce-entry-stage adults perceive their representative's effectiveness." },
          { name: "The 34% Opposition and Service Delivery Gaps", tag: "careful", why: "In a district that is 73% Latino with a 66-34 Democratic result, the opposition cannot be explained by demographic cross-pressures or competing identity coalitions—it reflects specific gaps between what this constituency needs and what the incumbent is delivering. The opposition may be concentrated among renters frustrated by displacement risk, among young adults perceiving limited economic opportunity, among parents evaluating school quality, or among voters who feel their needs are not being addressed relative to competing priorities. Diagnostically identifying where this 34% opposition comes from—and offering credible remedies—is the most direct path to both defending and expanding the seat." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Supermajority in a Homogeneous District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% Democratic result in a district that is 73% Latino, 43% renters, earning under $100k median income with a sub-20% college attainment rate should, on its demographic composition alone, produce a 70%+ Democratic margin. The gap between what the demographics suggest and what the actual result shows indicates that one-third of this constituency is either not aligned with the incumbent or perceiving unmet needs. Any candidate who treats 66% as a safe floor and consolidates only the existing coalition while ignoring what drove the 34% opposition will find the margin eroded in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Framing Housing or Economic Issues as 'Special Interests' Rather Than Material Constituent Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 43.4% are renters and median income is $95,225, framing tenant protection, affordable housing, or wage policy as special-interest advocacy rather than foundational economic security for the demographic majority alienates the constituency most directly served by those policies. A candidate who positions renter protection or education access as tangential rather than central will fail to mobilize the constituency most dependent on these issues and risk consolidating only the upper-income homeowner minority within a heavily Latino working-class district." }
