          { name: "Public Safety & Urban Quality-of-Life for High-Tax-Paying Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.2% college-educated, 39.3-year median age constituency paying high taxes in a high-cost urban market evaluates on whether public safety and homelessness interventions are visibly working — not on abstract commitment to root causes. A 63% Democratic margin built on educated voters depends on demonstrating that government services justify the tax burden; visible disorder and perception of ineffective homelessness response will erode this coalition faster than any other single factor." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement & Immigration Policy", tag: "careful", why: "At 39.25% of the district, the Hispanic and Latino community is nearly equal to the white plurality (39.48%) and is not automatically Democratic — working-class Latino families' priorities on economic mobility, immigration enforcement specifics, and education quality can diverge from college-educated white Democrats' priorities. Authentic engagement on these communities' material concerns, rather than treating the Hispanic vote as a secured Democratic block, is essential to sustaining the current coalition." },
          { name: "Characterizing High-Income District as Economically Vulnerable", tag: "avoid", why: "The $89,669 median household income sits above national median and can create messaging vulnerability if Democratic framing suggests the district is economically disadvantaged in absolute terms. The actual vulnerability is housing cost burden on renters and service-quality burden on high-tax payers — not broader economic hardship. Messaging that acknowledges affluence while identifying the specific cost pressures that constrain renters and tax-conscious households is more credible than framing the district as economically struggling." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Diversity Automatically Produces Democratic Outcomes", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic-white parity at 39% each, combined with meaningful Asian (11.91%) and Black (4.59%) populations, creates a genuinely mixed district where no group's interests dominate — and where credible Republican appeals on housing deregulation, public safety, or economic opportunity could plausibly reach 45-48% if Democratic governance on material issues is perceived as ineffective. The 63% margin reflects 2024 choices on governance quality, not demographic inevitability; treating it as secure invites a well-resourced challenger." }
