          { name: "Treating 75% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% result in a district with 73.4% renters and 63.1% college-educated voters is not a durable floor but a working margin that depends on active constituent service and visible legislative delivery. Renters are mobile, highly educated voters are politically sophisticated, and both groups evaluate on material outcomes (housing policy, education funding) rather than inherited partisan loyalty. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as secure and governs by consolidating the existing coalition, rather than deepening constituent service and visibly delivering on the specific concerns of renters and educated voters, risks a credible challenger who makes housing affordability or education funding a centerpiece and organizes these mobile constituencies." },
          { name: "Neglecting Constituent Service in Favor of Partisan Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 75-25 Democratic district with high political sophistication, the comparative advantage an incumbent holds is constituent service excellence and visible presence in neighborhood-level governance. Shifting focus to state or national partisan positioning, or allowing grassroots constituent service to atrophy, surrenders the incumbent's strongest asset—direct credibility with renters and educated voters who care most about local outcomes on housing, schools, and services. A challenger with no incumbent's record can campaign on housing affordability and education funding; an incumbent who is not visibly delivering on these issues has surrendered the one dimension on which they hold a comparative advantage." }
