          { name: "Healthcare Access & Medicaid Program Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.4% college-educated rate and working-class profile indicate employment primarily in service, construction, and light manufacturing sectors where employer healthcare coverage is often limited or expensive. Healthcare access and Medicaid program quality are direct economic security issues for families at $76,726 income with limited alternative coverage. Specific legislative delivery on healthcare expansion, Medicaid program quality, and prescription cost management speaks directly to how working families experience economic security or vulnerability." },
          { name: "Persuadable Working-Class Latino Voters & Economic Messaging", tag: "careful", why: "The 34% Republican vote in a 76.96% Latino, working-class district is anomalously high and suggests a subset of working-class Latino voters evaluating on economic competence and tangible delivery rather than inherited party loyalty. These voters are not lost; they are persuadable based on whether they perceive the legislator as actively solving the economic problems affecting their families. The incumbent's strategic vulnerability is assuming the 66% is a floor when it may actually reflect the upper bound of mobilization without continuous economic messaging and delivery." },
          { name: "Immigration-Centric Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "While HD-62 is a heavily Latino district, the demographic and income profile indicates a working-class constituency focused on wage growth, housing costs, and healthcare access — not immigration politics as the organizing issue. A campaign centered on immigration rhetoric or that treats Latino voters as a monolithic identity group around immigration will misdiagnose what actually drives voting behavior in this district and miss the opportunity to consolidate support around economic security issues." },
          { name: "Assuming the 66% Margin Is a Stable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this demographically aligned with Democratic support, a 66% result is not a strong floor — it is the incumbent's actual support level, and one-third of the constituency voting Republican despite favorable demographics is a warning signal about either economic dissatisfaction or organizational gaps in voter contact and turnout. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 66% as a safe margin and defaults to passive campaigning rather than active engagement on concrete economic issues risks finding the margin compressed significantly in a competitive cycle." }
