          { name: "Housing Affordability for Renters in an Expensive Market", tag: "careful", why: "At 47.9% renter rate and $99,848 median income, the district includes many working professionals and families who are cost-burdened despite relatively high nominal income. Rent control, tenant protections, and inclusionary zoning are politically contested in Orange County, and a representative's position on housing affordability can shift voting behavior among renters frustrated by displacement risk. This is a constituency that benefits from the incumbent's assumed Democratic positioning on housing, but one capable of moving to a challenger if housing costs become acute enough and the incumbent appears passive." },
          { name: "Using Racial or Economic Divisions to Fragment the Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "The electoral coalition that produced the 57% result requires genuine inclusion of Latino, Asian, and college-educated professional constituencies. A challenger or incumbent who campaigns by emphasizing divisions between these groups — framing Latino economic concerns against professional amenity priorities, or positioning immigration and workplace rights as competing with professional advancement — is not broadening but fracturing the coalition. The 57% is solid because these communities see their interests as shared." },
          { name: "Treating the 43% Opposition as Non-Constituent", tag: "avoid", why: "In an urban district with 47.9% renters, 35.1% college-educated residents, and three distinct demographic communities, the 43% opposition is not a monolithic conservative base but a coalition of affluent homeowners, business owners with different regulatory philosophies, and possibly working-class voters frustrated by incumbent governance on homelessness, public safety, or service delivery. A representative who campaigns exclusively to the 57% while dismissing the 43% as ideological opposition rather than constituent frustration misses the opportunity to consolidate a broader coalition and leaves the seat vulnerable to a challenger who addresses opposition concerns with specificity." }
