          "Engage the occupational economy directly: with 22.8% college attainment, the district's workforce is concentrated in service, trades, and manual labor sectors where wage stagnation and credential barriers constrain mobility; messaging on apprenticeships, prevailing wage, labor standards, and occupational licensing reform speaks to actual constituent circumstances rather than abstract economic policy.",
          "Build authentic relationships with specific minority communities: while Hispanic voters are the demographic majority, the Asian and Black communities represent constituencies whose healthcare access, small business support, and economic mobility concerns can become invisible if they are absorbed into a broader Hispanic narrative; allocate direct constituent engagement and policy attention proportional to their presence.",
          "Treat 64% as a floor to defend and expand, not a ceiling to assume: the 36% opposition and lower-than-statewide college attainment suggest a constituency that will evaluate candidates on concrete delivery around housing, wages, and labor protections; any incumbent or challenger who runs a mobilization-light campaign will risk the margin collapsing below 60% if a Republican candidate offers specific material arguments on cost of living or business climate."
