          { name: "Professional Licensing & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 29.3% college attainment and 38.17% Asian population, the district includes many skilled professionals and immigrant communities for whom professional licensing pathways—nursing, engineering, accounting, cosmetology, construction trades—are direct barriers to economic mobility. Licensing reform that recognizes foreign credentials, streamlines examination processes, and reduces occupational licensing barriers is concrete policy that serves the district's demographic majority and connects to their material economic interests. A legislator who advances these reforms demonstrates fluency in what actually constrains opportunity for this constituency." },
          { name: "Asian & Hispanic Community Policy Specificity", tag: "careful", why: "The 38.17% Asian and 33.87% Hispanic populations comprise 72% of the district, but within these categories exist distinct policy concerns—Southeast Asian, East Asian, and South Asian communities have different educational and business needs; Central American immigrant and multigenerational Hispanic households face different policy priorities around family immigration and labor protections. Generic 'diversity' outreach or undifferentiated minority-community appeals will be perceived as inauthentic by a constituency that is aware of internal policy variation; targeted engagement with specific communities and their particular policy concerns is required to maintain credibility." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result in a demographically diverse district with high income but high renter rates and real economic constraints is not a safe Republican seat in California—it is a contested seat that reflects incumbent incumbency and constituent relationships rather than stable partisan alignment. Any candidate who campaigns from a position of assumed security or who allows constituent engagement infrastructure to atrophy will find this margin rapidly compressed; the 45% represents a constituency that could consolidate around an alternative if they perceive insufficient responsiveness to their policy concerns." },
          { name: "Ignoring Internal Diversity Within Racial Categories", tag: "avoid", why: "A common strategic error in diverse districts is treating 'Asian' and 'Hispanic' as monolithic categories and deploying identical messaging to both. Southeast Asian refugees, East Asian professionals, South Asian immigrants, Central American families, multigenerational Mexican-Americans, and other communities within these categories have distinct policy concerns, historical relationships to government, and voting behavior. Any campaign that fails to engage this internal diversity—and instead relies on generic appeals to 'communities of color'—will be perceived as out-of-touch and will miss the opportunity to build genuine constituent relationships with the district's actual demographic composition." }
