          { name: "Anti-Growth Messaging & Housing Restriction as Democratic Vulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "In a high-income, college-educated district, Democratic opposition to housing development, aggressive environmental regulation that restricts building, or narratives framing growth as inherently destructive create an opening for Republican messaging about competence, opportunity, and whether government is solving problems or creating them. A Republican challenger in CA-HD-71 who leads on housing supply, business-friendly regulation, and economic growth can credibly position themselves as the pro-opportunity candidate, particularly if the Democrat is perceived as hostile to development. Any Democratic messaging or policy that allows this framing to take hold — whether through actual votes on development or rhetoric about growth — activates the 39% Republican opposition and opens the seat." },
          { name: "Assuming the 62% Margin Is Structural Rather Than Earned", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62% Democratic result in a California district is significant, but in a constituency this affluent and educated, the margin reflects current alignment between Democratic governance and voter preferences — not permanent party loyalty. High-income, college-educated voters are capable of evaluating candidates on competence and results, not ideology; if Democratic governance is perceived as failing on schools, local services, or housing affordability while taxes rise, that 62% can contract rapidly. Any representative who treats the margin as a safe foundation and neglects active constituent engagement, measurable service delivery improvement, or response to the 39% opposition is at risk of finding the seat unexpectedly competitive in a subsequent cycle." }
