          { name: "Education Quality & Investment as Direct Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 39.8% college attainment, a large share of CA-SD-2's constituency has or will have children in K-12 schools and views education funding as a direct personal investment with immediate household returns, not as a charitable social commitment. A representative who engages education quality, teacher compensation, school infrastructure, and curriculum specificity — and connects these directly to the district's property values and professional family stability — speaks the language of a constituency that understands education as foundational to household economic security and community reputation." },
          { name: "Generational Replacement & Inherited Democratic Loyalty", tag: "careful", why: "The 73% Democratic margin reflects consolidation of an educated, affluent, professional-class base whose Democratic alignment is rational and interest-based rather than inherited. Younger adults entering this district or aging into peak earning years within it may not automatically maintain this allegiance if they perceive Democratic governance as economically inefficient, excessively regulatory, or inattentive to their material concerns — particularly around housing costs and childcare access. A holding strategy requires active constituent service and economic delivery, not assumption that the demographic base will perpetuate inherited loyalty without proof of value." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 27% Republican Constituency as Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this dominant, the Republican 27% can appear electorally irrelevant and thus easy to ignore or dismiss. However, this constituency likely includes legitimate grievances around business regulation, cost of local government, or specific governance failures that remain unaddressed in a Democratic supermajority context. A representative who treats this 27% as an opposition force to be overcome rather than as a source of policy feedback on cost management and regulatory efficiency risks missing valid governance insights and deepens the perception that Democratic dominance permits governance complacency." },
          { name: "Complacency About the 73% and Reliance on Partisan Identity", tag: "avoid", why: "The most dangerous strategic error in CA-SD-2 is treating the 73% as a permanent structural advantage that permits focus on partisan messaging, ideological positioning, or personal brand-building disconnected from economic delivery to the constituency. The 73% reflects alignment between the district's material interests and Democratic governance — not tribal Democratic loyalty. If economic conditions shift, if housing costs spike, if the incumbent becomes perceived as inattentive to service delivery, or if a primary challenger emerges who addresses material concerns more concretely, that 73% can erode quickly. A holding strategy requires constant attention to the economic fundamentals that make this constituency Democratic, not confidence that the margin is structural rather than contingent." }
