          "Build across the district's four-way demographic split (no group above 32%) by addressing shared economic concerns rather than ethnic or racial appeals — housing, transportation, public safety, and cost-of-living pressure affect all four communities simultaneously, and a candidate who speaks to shared material interests outperforms one who manages coalitions through identity-based positioning.",
          "Engage the 50.3% college-educated constituency on governance specificity and policy analysis: this is a highly educated, information-rich electorate that evaluates candidates on the coherence and sophistication of their policy proposals, not on slogans or inherited partisan loyalty — detailed positions on housing policy, transportation, and fiscal management will perform significantly better than generic party messaging.",
          "Recognize that 57% is a working margin, not a stable floor: the 43% opposition in a high-income, educated urban district signals real dissatisfaction with incumbent performance on material issues like housing costs, public order, and quality-of-life delivery — any candidate who consolidates the existing Democratic vote without winning back defectors among educated professionals and renters frustrated by cost-of-living pressure will find this seat genuinely competitive."
