          { name: "Treating 69% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 margin in California is not a safe seat — it reflects a specific moment in a rapidly shifting state. The incumbent's 69% result is concentrated among white homeowners and does not reflect durable support among the district's 34.93% Hispanic and 8.5% Asian population, or among the 34.3% renter constituency that faces acute economic pressure. Any candidate who campaigns as though 69% is a structural majority rather than an incumbent advantage that depends on suppressed opposition turnout among renters and communities of color will be vulnerable to a credible challenger who activates these constituencies around their material concerns." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 31% Opposition as a Localized Problem", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Democrats received 31% in 2022, a 69-31 margin is meaningful but not overwhelming — and the opposing coalition's size and composition matter strategically. The 31% represents the district's renter majority, Hispanic and Latino households, Asian Americans, and younger adults whose economic concerns (housing, healthcare, wage mobility) are sharpening rather than softening over time. Treating this opposition as incidental rather than as a core constituency demanding specific answers about economic security and opportunity will leave a challenger the opening to mobilize around these concrete concerns and erode support among persuadable renters and Hispanic voters." }
