        memoHeadline: "San Diego's Affluent Coast: 69% Republican in a High-Income District Facing Housing Affordability Pressure, a 35% Hispanic Constituency, and a 34% Renter Swing Vote",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "CA-SD-12 is a high-income coastal San Diego district where the 69% Republican margin obscures significant economic stress and demographic diversity beneath the aggregate affluence. The $88,161 median household income and 31.1% college attainment reflect an educated, financially stable population on the surface, but the 34.3% renter rate — substantially higher than typical wealthy suburban districts — reveals a constituency for whom income and housing costs are structurally misaligned. In coastal California's market, $88K median income renting households are economically precarious despite appearing affluent on paper; they are a swing constituency vulnerable to messaging about cost of living, housing stability, and whether government is actually delivering for working families. The incumbent's 69% victory rests on consolidating white homeowner support and suppressing opposition turnout among renters and minority communities — a strategy that is durable only if opposition constituencies remain unactivated.",
          "The district's 34.93% Hispanic and Latino population and 8.5% Asian American population together represent over 43% of residents and are the source of the opposing 31% Democratic result. These communities have distinct economic concerns — Latino working families navigating housing costs and healthcare access, Asian American entrepreneurs managing small business constraints and regulatory burden, both groups seeking economic mobility in a high-cost region — that are not naturally aligned with either major party but are strategically available to whoever offers specific answers to their material conditions. The 31% opposition is not a ceiling but a floor for any candidate who activates these constituencies around their concrete concerns about affordability, healthcare, and opportunity. Demographic changes, generational turnover among younger adults more exposed to Democratic messaging, and rising housing pressure make CA-SD-12 less durable as a 69% Republican seat than the current margin suggests — the critical variable is whether the opposing coalition remains fragmented or becomes mobilized around shared economic concerns."
        ],
