          { name: "Latino Community Voter Mobilization & Retention", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 66.43% Latino population is the electoral majority in CA-SD-14, yet the 56% Democratic margin in a state where coastal urbanization typically produces larger Democratic gains indicates that Latino voter turnout and candidate preference are actively contestable. Latino voters in this district are economically stressed, young, and pragmatic — they will mobilize for candidates who deliver material improvements in wages, housing, and healthcare access, and they will disengage or shift support if they conclude politicians are taking their vote for granted. Sustained community engagement, Spanish-language organizing, and demonstrated policy delivery on Latino-specific priorities determine whether this margin holds or expands." },
          { name: "Young Adult Economic Mobility & Wage Growth", tag: "careful", why: "With a 31.5-year median age and only 14.3% college attainment, CA-SD-14 is a young, working-class constituency that evaluates candidates on economic opportunity rather than inherited partisan loyalty. Job quality, wage growth, workforce development programs, and access to skills training are the material governance concerns for voters at this age and education level. Young adults in this district are capable of shifting support if they perceive a credible alternative path to economic stability — making this constituency both a Democratic strength (if mobilized around economic delivery) and a potential vulnerability (if neglected or taken for granted)." },
          { name: "Assuming Latino Turnout Is Automatic", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56% Democratic margin in a heavily Latino, working-class district in California suggests that Latino voter participation is not guaranteed by demographic category — it is an active choice contingent on candidates demonstrating responsiveness to economic priorities. Any Democratic candidate who campaigns as though the Latino vote is automatically theirs, rather than building specific community relationships and making concrete commitments on housing, wages, and immigration status protections, will find turnout soften when voters perceive indifference to their material concerns. Latino voters in working-class districts are pragmatists, not ideology voters." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "In a renter-majority, young, working-class district with volatile turnout patterns and pragmatic voter behavior, the 56% Democratic margin is not a demographic lock — it is an active choice by economically stressed voters who are evaluating whether government serves their interests. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 56% represents a stable base rather than an outcome contingent on delivering material improvements in housing, wages, and economic opportunity will find that margin collapse when turnout drops or when challengers offer more credible economic alternatives. This district demands active constituent engagement and demonstrated policy delivery, not inherited partisan advantage." }
