          { name: "Non-Credential Economic Mobility & Skilled Workforce Pathways", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's median household income of $86,606 reflects economic security, but only 23.2% of adults hold college degrees — a significant gap that reveals the majority of prosperity rests on trades, skilled service work, small business, and vocational expertise rather than credential-dependent employment. A candidate who can articulate a concrete vision for apprenticeships, vocational training, licensing reform, and professional-wage jobs accessible without four-year degrees is addressing the actual economic pathway of most households in the district. This is not a working-class messaging play; it is recognition of how the district's actual economy functions and what voters in this income-education intersection need from policy." },
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Economic Concerns & Small-Business Support", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 63.4% of the district, Hispanic voters are the demographic majority, but their 59% Democratic support in 2022 suggests internal diversity rather than monolithic loyalty — particularly when 42% of the district voted Republican. Hispanic voters in a $86,606-income district are likely evaluating on economic management, small-business support, labor-market access, and government effectiveness as much as cultural issues. A candidate who can speak specifically to immigrant-entrepreneurship support, labor-contractor licensing, commercial real-estate access, and neighborhood economic development will outperform one who defaults to ethnic-identity assumptions about partisan preference." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Constituency Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "The 42.1% renter rate in a $86,606-income district signals that San Diego County's cost-of-living pressures are real and material for a substantial minority of households, even those above-median income. Housing affordability is a legitimate policy concern for renters in this economic bracket, but it must be addressed with specificity about zoning, supply constraints, and rent-stabilization approaches rather than as a proxy for ideological positioning. A candidate who treats renter concerns as genuine economic issues rather than as partisan rhetoric will find this constituency more responsive than one who dismisses housing costs as reflecting individual choices rather than structural constraint." },
          { name: "Treating the 42% Republican Vote as Ideological Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "In a $86,606-income district with 63% Hispanic and 17% Asian population, the 42% Republican vote is not concentrated among a culturally conservative base but rather represents meaningful numbers of voters from the district's largest ethnic communities evaluating on economic competence and government effectiveness. Any Democratic candidate who campaigns as though Republicans in CA-SD-22 are culturally motivated rather than economically motivated will misread the actual persuasion opportunity and alienate voters who could be responsive to a different set of policy arguments." },
          { name: "Assuming College Attainment as a Prerequisite for Economic Respectability", tag: "avoid", why: "At 23.2% college attainment in a $86,606-income district, the implicit assumption that education requires a four-year degree is both demographically false and politically damaging — it positions as economically marginal the majority of households whose prosperity rests on other pathways. Any candidate whose economic messaging defaults to college-degree emphasis or treats vocational work as a fallback option will actively alienate the district's actual economic majority and signal misunderstanding of how their constituent base actually achieves security." }
