          { name: "Renter & Younger Adult Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 33.2% renter rate and a 35-year median age, CA-SD-23 has a substantial constituency of younger adults and families evaluating candidates on whether government delivers material improvements to their lives. Housing affordability, education quality, healthcare costs, and wage growth are not secondary issues for this demographic — they are the primary household budget concerns that determine voter preference. A candidate who offers concrete policy answers to renter economic security and younger adult opportunity — zoning reform, education funding, healthcare access — addresses the most active swing voters in the district." },
          { name: "Understanding the 48% Opposition in a Competitive Margin", tag: "careful", why: "A 48% opposition result in a district this demographically favorable to Democratic performance (49% Latino, 33% renters) indicates that the opposition is not a floor but a competitive constituency with legitimate grievances or alternative preferences. Whether driven by economic anxiety among working-class households skeptical of government, specific local governance failures, or candidate-specific concerns, understanding precisely what generated 48% opposition is essential to consolidating the district. A candidate who assumes the Democratic base will show up without addressing opposition concerns will find the margin collapse in a higher-friction cycle." },
          { name: "Treating the White 30% as a Monolithic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "At 30.09% white in a district with $86,836 median income and 33.2% renters, the white community is not uniformly homeowner-conservative; it includes renters, younger adults, and working-class households whose economic interests may align with the Latino-plurality and renter-majority constituencies around housing, wages, and education. Any candidate whose strategy treats the white 30% as a unified conservative base while neglecting the economic interests they share with Latino and younger voters will fail to consolidate even the constituencies most naturally aligned with their party." },
          { name: "Generic Minority Outreach Without Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "In a Latino-plurality district with real housing pressures, education access concerns, and wage growth expectations, generic messaging about 'supporting Latino communities' or diversity without specific policy answers to household budget concerns will not move Latino voter preference or turnout meaningfully. A candidate who campaigns on abstract values without concrete answers to housing affordability, healthcare access, or education funding in a district where 33% are renters and median income is $86,836 is neglecting the material issues that actually drive Latino voter behavior in a competitive district." }
