          { name: "Housing Affordability and Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 55.4% renters at $88,616 median income, a substantial portion of CA-SD-34's constituency is cost-burdened, spending more than 30% of income on rent and unable to save, invest in education, or build wealth. Housing costs rising faster than wages is the material daily constraint on economic security for the majority of district households. Any candidate who does not lead with housing policy — rent stabilization, tenant protections, zoning reform for supply, and habitability enforcement — is not addressing the primary economic concern that shapes voting behavior in this district." },
          { name: "Economic Mobility and Educational Access for Working-Sector Majority", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 23.8% college attainment and 34.9 median age in a 64.56% Latino, 16.42% Asian district, CA-SD-34's constituency is concentrated in service and working-sector employment where educational pathways and wealth-building assets are not yet accessible or embedded in family experience. Community college affordability, vocational training, apprenticeships, and wage standards in service sectors are the concrete policies that respond to a constituency whose economic mobility depends on accessible education and fair compensation, not on inherited credentials or family assets." },
          { name: "Immigrant Community Economic Exploitation and Labor Standards", tag: "careful", why: "The 64.56% Latino population in CA-SD-34 includes significant immigrant and immigrant-origin communities who face wage theft, workplace exploitation, and wage-suppression through immigration enforcement that creates a fearful workforce. Enforcement of labor standards, protection from retaliation for wage claims, and workplace safety in sectors where immigrants concentrate — agriculture, food service, home care, construction — are specific policy areas where the incumbent's record and visibility matter directly to whether the Latino community sees their interests as represented." },
          { name: "Treating the 59% as a Durable Floor Rather Than a Competitive Signal", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59% Democratic result in a district that is 64.56% Latino, 16.42% Asian, 55.4% renter, and 23.8% college-educated should be producing a 65-70% Democratic margin if the constituency believed their economic needs were being met. The gap between that expectation and the current 59% is the most important strategic fact — it signals either incumbent underperformance on economic delivery or effective opposition messaging on cost-of-living concerns. Any candidate who assumes 59% is safe and does not actively address what generated the 41% opposition will find that margin compressed." },
          { name: "Generic Diversity or Multicultural Rhetoric Without Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency that is 64.56% Latino and 16.42% Asian does not need affirmation of diversity — it needs specific policies addressing the material economic concerns of working families in those communities. Generic multiculturalism without housing policy, without wage standards, without educational access, and without labor enforcement reads as performative to constituents evaluating on whether their rent is affordable and whether their wages are sufficient to support their families. The 41% opposition in a district this demographically aligned with Democratic positions suggests that messaging without material policy backing is not sufficient." }
