          { name: "Coalition Depth Across Hispanic, Asian, and White Pluralities", tag: "lean-into", why: "CA-SD-39 is a genuine three-way demographic district: Hispanic 30.46%, White 44.55%, and Asian 11.07%, with no single group commanding a majority. The 63-37 Democratic margin reflects coalition strength across all three communities, not dominance within any one. Understanding what specifically drives Hispanic voter support (economic mobility, healthcare access, education), what matters to Asian constituents (small business environment, education quality, property rights), and what keeps the 44.55% white plurality aligned is essential to both holding the seat and understanding where vulnerability might emerge if coalition cohesion falters." },
          { name: "College-Educated Constituency Expectations & Professional Class Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "At 40.8% college-educated adults, CA-SD-39 has a substantial professional and educated-worker constituency whose governance expectations diverge from both working-class renters and non-college homeowners. This group tends to evaluate on quality governance, policy competence, environmental regulation, and public investment in education and infrastructure—not necessarily partisan loyalty. If the incumbent is perceived as inadequate on execution or competence, this 40.8% can become persuadable or stay home; conversely, a challenger who speaks to professional-class concerns with specificity can peel off meaningful support from the current coalition." },
          { name: "Treating the 63% as a Durable Coalition Rather Than an Unstable Plurality", tag: "avoid", why: "The 63-37 margin in California State Senate elections reflects a coalition of renters, Hispanic voters, college-educated professionals, and younger adults who have aligned behind Democratic representation—but this is not a 63% base of inherited partisan loyalty in a red state. In a purple California district with 54.3% renters under housing pressure, a meaningful college-educated minority, and three demographic communities with distinct economic interests, the coalition requires active maintenance. An incumbent who assumes the 63% will hold without addressing specific constituent concerns about housing, education quality, or economic mobility will discover that coalition cohesion is more fragile than the margin suggests." },
          { name: "Attributing Weakness to Constituency Ideology Rather Than Incumbent Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "The 36.1 median age and 40.8% college education suggest a constituency that evaluates candidates substantially on perceived competence, constituent service quality, and whether government is delivering tangible results—not on abstract ideological alignment. Any candidate who loses ground in CA-SD-39 and attributes it to the district becoming 'too conservative' or 'lacking appetite for progressive policy' is misdiagnosing the problem; the district's actual concern is whether the representative is solving housing costs, improving school quality, and making government work for renters and working professionals. Ideology framing obscures the actual governance issues that drive voter behavior in this district." }
