          { name: "Assuming a Six-Point Margin is a Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "At 53% Republican in a district where the demographic majority consists of Asian and Latino voters, the incumbent's margin is dependent on specific alignment between their profile and the white homeowner coalition—and on the assumption that Asian and Latino constituencies will not mobilize at higher rates or consolidate around an alternative. In a high-income, educated district with 45% college attainment and significant demographic diversity, a six-point margin is genuinely vulnerable to a challenger who offers credible governance on the issues that matter to the demographic majority. Any incumbent who treats 53% as a floor rather than a contested result is underestimating the district's actual competitiveness." }
